# How does solving x' = A(t)x scale with system size?

This benchmark addresses [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#404](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404):
for a general non-autonomous linear system `x'(t) = A(t) x(t)`, (i) how does wall-clock
time and memory to solve it scale with the system size `N`, and (ii) how large an `N x N`
system (with a non-trivial number of time steps, up to millions) can be solved on a single
machine? As
[noted in the issue thread](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404#issuecomment-1019531233),
`x' = A(t)x` is structurally different from a generic ODE: specialized **Magnus and
matrix-exponential (Lie group) integrators** exist specifically for this class of problem.
Per that same comment, this is deliberately scoped to a single machine (no multi-node/MPI),
and the test problem is built exactly as suggested there: a method-of-lines discretization
of a diffusion PDE, so this also illustrates how solve cost scales as a PDE is discretized
more finely in space.

An earlier version of this page compared methods at mismatched, arbitrarily-chosen
tolerances (a fixed `dt` for the exponential integrator against a fixed `reltol` for
`Tsit5`) and tested only the generic `LinearExponential` wrapper, not the actual Magnus
family or the stabilized explicit (ROCK) methods designed for exactly this class of
mildly-stiff, parabolic problem. This revision fixes both: it uses proper **work-precision
diagrams** (time to reach a given, matched accuracy, the standard methodology used
throughout this repository) and includes `MagnusGL4`, `MagnusGL6`, and the adaptive
`MagnusAdapt4` from `OrdinaryDiffEqLinear`, alongside `ROCK2`/`ROCK4` from
`OrdinaryDiffEqStabilizedRK`.

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEqLinear, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5, OrdinaryDiffEqVerner, OrdinaryDiffEqStabilizedRK
using SciMLOperators, LinearAlgebra, SparseArrays
using DiffEqDevTools
using BenchmarkTools, Plots, Printf
gr(fmt = :png)
```

## The model: a diffusion equation with time-varying diffusivity

`du/dt = kappa(t) * u_xx` on `[0, 1]` with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions and
`kappa(t) = 1 + 0.5*sin(t)` (bounded, strictly positive, so the problem stays well-posed for
all `t`). Discretizing `u_xx` with a standard second-order central difference on `N`
interior grid points gives exactly a linear, non-autonomous ODE system `x'(t) = A(t) x(t)`,
with `A(t) = kappa(t) * L`, `L` the (fixed, tridiagonal) discrete Laplacian scaled by
`1/dx^2`. This is precisely the "continually discretize the PDE in space" scaling question
raised in the issue. `dense` selects a `Matrix` (needed by the Magnus family and by
`LinearExponential(krylov = :off)`, both of which compute an exact matrix exponential via
`exp`) or a `SparseMatrixCSC` (needed for the large-`N` Krylov section below, since `exp` of
a sparse matrix is not defined).

```julia
function laplacian_operator(N)
    dx = 1.0 / (N + 1)
    L = (1 / dx^2) .* spdiagm(-1 => fill(1.0, N - 1), 0 => fill(-2.0, N), 1 => fill(1.0, N - 1))
    return L, dx
end

function linear_problem(N; tspan = (0.0, 0.1), dense = false)
    L, dx = laplacian_operator(N)
    L = dense ? Matrix(L) : L
    update_func!(A, u, p, t) = (A .= (1 + 0.5 * sin(t)) .* L)
    A = MatrixOperator(copy(L); update_func! = update_func!)
    u0 = [sin(pi * i * dx) for i in 1:N]
    return ODEProblem(A, u0, tspan), L, u0
end
```

Every algorithm below -- `Tsit5`, `Vern7`, `ROCK2`, `ROCK4`, the Magnus methods, and
`LinearExponential` -- solves this *same* `ODEProblem` object directly (an
`AbstractSciMLOperator` is callable as `A(u, p, t)`, so any generic solver works on it, not
just the specialized ones), which is what makes the comparison below apples-to-apples.

## Correctness check

```julia
N_check = 200
prob_check, _, _ = linear_problem(N_check)

sol_exp = solve(prob_check, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
sol_tsit5 = solve(prob_check, Tsit5(), reltol = 1e-10, abstol = 1e-10)

println("max abs difference: ", maximum(abs.(sol_exp.u[end] .- sol_tsit5.u[end])))
```

The two solutions agree to within the truncation error implied by `LinearExponential`'s
fixed `dt = 0.001` (it must resolve `kappa(t)`'s variation using its own step size, unlike
`Tsit5`'s adaptive, tightly-toleranced stepping) -- more than sufficient to confirm the
problem is set up correctly before comparing work-precision below.

## Work-precision diagrams: general-purpose vs. stabilized vs. Magnus/exponential

A fair comparison has to hold accuracy fixed and measure cost, not compare methods at
tolerances or step sizes chosen independently for each one. We build a reference solution
with a high-order adaptive method at very tight tolerance, then sweep each candidate method
across a range of its own tolerance (or, for the fixed-step Magnus/exponential methods,
`dt`) and plot the resulting (time, error) pairs.

```julia
function reference_solution(prob)
    sol = solve(prob, Vern9(), abstol = 1e-13, reltol = 1e-13)
    return TestSolution(sol)
end

abstols = 0.1 .^ (2:6)
reltols = 0.1 .^ (2:6)
dts = [0.1, 0.05, 0.025, 0.0125, 0.00625]

setups = [
    Dict(:alg => Tsit5()),
    Dict(:alg => Vern7()),
    Dict(:alg => ROCK2()),
    Dict(:alg => ROCK4()),
    Dict(:alg => MagnusGL4(), :dts => dts),
    Dict(:alg => MagnusGL6(), :dts => dts),
    Dict(:alg => MagnusAdapt4()),
    Dict(:alg => LinearExponential(krylov = :off), :dts => dts),
]
labels = hcat("Tsit5", "Vern7", "ROCK2", "ROCK4", "MagnusGL4", "MagnusGL6", "MagnusAdapt4", "LinearExponential")
```

`MagnusGL4`/`MagnusGL6` are genuine Magnus expansions (they integrate `A(t)`'s
time-dependence to 4th/6th order within each step via nested commutators), `MagnusAdapt4`
is their adaptive-step sibling, and `LinearExponential(krylov = :off)` instead *freezes*
`A(t)` at the start of each step and takes its exact matrix exponential -- cheap per step,
but only first-order accurate in how it resolves `kappa(t)`'s time-variation. `ROCK2`/`ROCK4`
are stabilized (Runge-Kutta-Chebyshev) explicit methods, purpose-built for exactly this
mildly-stiff, real-negative-eigenvalue regime that a diffusion operator produces. We omit
`MagnusGL8`: at the grid sizes below it already sits at the same machine-precision floor as
`MagnusGL4`/`MagnusGL6` on this smooth, slowly-varying `kappa(t)`, so it adds cost without
adding information.

### Small system (N=30)

```julia
N_small = 30
prob_small = linear_problem(N_small; dense = true)[1]
test_sol_small = reference_solution(prob_small)

wp_small = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_small, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = labels, print_names = true, numruns = 1, error_estimate = :l2,
    save_everystep = false, appxsol = test_sol_small, maxiters = Int(1e7))

plot(wp_small, title = "Work-Precision, N=$N_small", legend = :outertopright, size = (850, 500))
```

### Larger system (N=300)

```julia
N_large = 300
prob_large = linear_problem(N_large; dense = true)[1]
test_sol_large = reference_solution(prob_large)

wp_large = WorkPrecisionSet(prob_large, abstols, reltols, setups;
    names = labels, print_names = true, numruns = 1, error_estimate = :l2,
    save_everystep = false, appxsol = test_sol_large, maxiters = Int(1e7))

plot(wp_large, title = "Work-Precision, N=$N_large", legend = :outertopright, size = (850, 500))
```

### Reading the two diagrams

At `N=30` every method is fast in absolute terms, but the Magnus methods are already the
standout: `MagnusGL4`/`MagnusGL6` reach errors nine to twelve orders of magnitude smaller
than `Tsit5`/`Vern7`/`ROCK2`/`ROCK4` for comparable or *less* wall-clock time.
`LinearExponential` is consistently worse than the real Magnus methods at every matched
step size -- direct evidence that "exactly exponentiate a frozen operator" is not a good
substitute for actually integrating the time-dependence, which is exactly what the Magnus
expansion is for.

At `N=300`, the discrete Laplacian's stiffness (`~N^2`) has grown enough that `Tsit5` and
`Vern7`'s stability-restricted step size makes every solve take **seconds** regardless of
requested tolerance, while `ROCK2`/`ROCK4` -- designed to take large stable steps through
exactly this kind of stiffness -- are 20-100x faster for comparable accuracy. The Magnus
methods and `LinearExponential` also slow down substantially at `N=300`: they rely on a
*dense* matrix exponential (or commutator) each step, which costs `O(N^3)`, so their raw
wall-clock time is no longer obviously better than `Tsit5`'s despite still reaching far
higher accuracy per step. The honest picture is nuanced rather than a single method
"winning": Magnus methods dominate on accuracy-per-step, ROCK dominates on raw wall-clock
scalability without any dense linear algebra, and plain adaptive explicit RK is competitive
only while `N` (and hence the stiffness) stays small.

## Pushing system size as far as practical on a single machine

The dense matrix exponential/commutators that make the Magnus family so accurate above are
an `O(N^3)` cost that makes them impractical much past a few hundred unknowns. To push `N`
into the tens of thousands (directly answering part (ii) of the issue), we switch to
`LinearExponential(krylov = :simple)` on the *sparse* Laplacian: it approximates the same
matrix-exponential action with a low-dimensional Krylov subspace, at `O(N)`-ish cost per
step rather than `O(N^3)`, trading some of the accuracy shown above for scalability that
none of the dense methods above (Magnus included) can offer.

```julia
Ns_push = [1_000, 5_000, 10_000, 25_000, 50_000]
times_push = Float64[]
mem_push = Float64[]

for N in Ns_push
    local probA
    probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N)
    t = @elapsed sol = solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
    b = @allocated solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = 0.001)
    push!(times_push, t); push!(mem_push, b / 1e6)
    @printf("N=%-6d  time=%8.3fs   memory=%8.2fMB\n", N, t, b / 1e6)
end
```

```julia
plot(Ns_push, times_push, marker = :circle, xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "N", ylabel = "wall-clock time (s)",
    title = "Sparse Krylov LinearExponential: pushing system size", legend = false, size = (700, 450))
```

## Scaling with the number of time steps

The issue also asks about pushing the number of time steps into the thousands-to-millions
range. Since each Krylov `LinearExponential` step is an independent exponential-action
computation (rather than something whose cost depends on how many steps came before it),
total cost is expected to grow roughly in proportion to the number of steps taken, up to
run-to-run variance in exactly how many Krylov iterations a given step needs to converge.

```julia
N_fixed = 200
probA, _, _ = linear_problem(N_fixed)
n_steps_list = [10^3, 10^4, 10^5, 10^6]
times_steps = Float64[]

for n_steps in n_steps_list
    dt = 0.1 / n_steps
    t = @elapsed solve(probA, LinearExponential(krylov = :simple, m = 30), dt = dt)
    push!(times_steps, t)
    @printf("n_steps=%-9d  time=%8.3fs   (%.2e s/step)\n", n_steps, t, t / n_steps)
end
```

```julia
plot(n_steps_list, times_steps, marker = :circle, xscale = :log10, yscale = :log10,
    xlabel = "number of time steps", ylabel = "wall-clock time (s)",
    title = "LinearExponential: scaling with step count (N=$N_fixed)",
    legend = false, size = (700, 450))
```

# Conclusion

For a non-autonomous linear system `x' = A(t)x` built from a method-of-lines PDE
discretization, matched-accuracy work-precision comparisons (rather than time at
mismatched, arbitrarily-chosen tolerances) show a genuinely three-way trade-off, not a
single winner: the true Magnus family (`MagnusGL4`, `MagnusGL6`, `MagnusAdapt4`) integrates
the time-dependence to high order and dominates on accuracy-per-step at small-to-moderate
`N`; the stabilized explicit `ROCK2`/`ROCK4` methods avoid any dense linear algebra and so
scale far better in raw wall-clock time as the discretization's stiffness (`~N^2`) grows;
and the generic `LinearExponential` "freeze-and-exponentiate" approach, while exact for a
frozen operator, is consistently less accurate than the real Magnus methods at matched cost
-- it is best understood as a *sparse-Krylov-compatible* fallback for scaling to very large
`N`, not as the most accurate choice at moderate `N`. That fallback still answers part (ii)
of the issue directly: `LinearExponential(krylov = :simple)` on a sparse Laplacian reaches
tens of thousands of unknowns and up to a million time steps in a non-trivial but tractable
amount of wall-clock time on a single machine. Per the
[issue discussion](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/404), this is
intentionally single-node in scope; extending it to distributed/HPC-scale problems (and to
other PDE benchmarks such as Brusselator or Gray-Scott, as suggested there) is a natural,
larger follow-up.

